# The lock screen's fingerprint check, used when the system has no
# /etc/pam.d/h2s-shell-fingerprint (and not on NixOS, where a module named without a path
# is looked for only in libpam's own directory: the NixOS module writes the system-wide
# one). It runs beside the password check, not before it.
#
# A passive check: it must never reach a module that asks for typed input (pam_unix…).
# The shell treats a passive check that asks for input as broken and never answers it —
# an empty answer in a loop would be a failed password attempt each time. So
# pam_fprintd alone, `required`: its result (success, failure, MaxTries, a reader not
# there) is the result.
#
# `timeout=-1`: no time limit on waiting for a finger (pam_fprintd: a negative timeout is
# none). Its default, 30 s, ends an untouched round with an error, every 30 s for as long as
# the screen is locked; the shell ends the conversation itself — at the unlock, when the
# lock ends, before the machine sleeps. `max-tries` is pam_fprintd's own (3 fingers that do
# not match, then MaxTries; the shell waits a moment and starts again).
#
# Quickshell runs pam_authenticate and nothing else: only `auth` lines mean anything.
# The first line only says that PAM got going (pam_echo's message, never shown, changes no
# result): libpam loads every module before it runs, and a check that has not said it
# within a few seconds is stuck before any module ran, and is started again. Keep it first.
auth     [default=ignore] pam_echo.so h2s-shell-lock.started
auth     required pam_fprintd.so timeout=-1
